{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 272 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "77fe235e7a715de62defd414a0511d8561555c285537cc5e06a937a0cba86b27" :edb/db-before-t 271 :edb/db-after-t 272 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533584 50 #inst "2026-10-11T10:32:30.034Z" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186091944 1071 "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186091944 1071 "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186091944 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]$.\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2$, and $5$ are mapped to $1$. Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,5 \\}\n$$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186091944 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186091945 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n\n- $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186091945 1071 "$\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n2. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n3. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186091945 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A}$, $A \\cap B$, and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x) \\\\\n1_{A∩B}(x) &= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)} \\\\\n1_{A∪B}(x) &= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A∪B}(-1) &= max{1_{A}(-1), 1_{B}(-1)} \\\\\n&= max{0, 1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$, we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186091945 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)} \\\\  & =max{0,1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409889 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409892 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409888 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409890 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409891 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409893 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092441 1050 420.0 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089771 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186092600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409887 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092600 1071 "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U = {a, b, c, d, e}$ whose indicator function $1_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186092600 1071 "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U={a,b,c,d,e}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092600 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)={\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A={a,b,d}.$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089783 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186092602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409882 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092602 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n- $5\\in A\\cap B$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186092602 1071 "$1_{A}(5) = 1, 1_{B}(5) = 0$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $1_{A∩B}(5) = 1$\n2. $1_{A∩B}(5) = 0$\n3. $5 ∈ A∩B$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186092602 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5) & =min{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)} \\\\  & =min{1,0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0,$ we conclude that $5$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186093495 1064 "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,c \\}\n$$\n\nbe a subset of the universal set\n\n$$\nX=\\{a,b,c,d,e \\}\n$$\n\nThen, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1$, and all other elements are mapped to $0$." 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186093495 1064 "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/af/af268dd04b20dde1e7216e63839f309f572615b59f57f81515cd2e7998c1f45d.svg)\n\nFor example, let $A = \\{ a,c \\}$ be a subset of the universal set $X=\\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.$ Then, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186093496 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186093496 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in \\overline{A}[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin \\overline{A}[/math]\n$$\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B$.\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have:\n$1_{A∩B}(x)|= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have\n$1_{A∪B}(x)|= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) + 1_{B}(x) - 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$.\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." 13194139533584 17592186045417 false] [17592186409840 1080 "III only" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409840 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409840 1083 #uuid "06dd1182-e612-5525-b9de-c12d2d05c3e6" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409841 1080 "{a,c,e}" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409841 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409841 1083 #uuid "1325aba8-981f-532b-b892-12eb6305d32e" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409842 1080 "I only" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409842 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409842 1083 #uuid "14622146-9357-5002-bb58-2d5df436755d" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409843 1080 "{-3,-1,0}" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409843 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409843 1083 #uuid "1682b7b8-dbb8-5c5c-8816-d4c1a452d7d3" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409844 1080 "II only" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409844 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409844 1083 #uuid "1737c9b3-9afe-57ff-999b-4fa97a61723d" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409845 1080 "II and III only" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409845 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409845 1083 #uuid "1a0cc322-449d-5a1c-a8d7-1039e1ba0529" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409846 1080 "{a,b,d}" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409846 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409846 1083 #uuid "2fd26958-8f1d-57a3-abb9-807967b6f0e5" 13194139533584 17592186045417 true] [17592186409847 1080 "{b,c,e}" 13194139533584